### What is the default message size?

Kafka's default maximum message size is approximately 1,048,576 bytes (1 MB). This limit exists at multiple layers and all of them matter.

```java
package com.bank.config;

public class DefaultMessageSize {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Default message size limits:");
        System.out.println("  Broker  message.max.bytes        : 1048588 bytes (~1 MB)");
        System.out.println("  Topic   max.message.bytes         : inherits broker default");
        System.out.println("  Producer max.request.size          : 1048576 bytes (1 MB)");
        System.out.println("  Consumer max.partition.fetch.bytes : 1048576 bytes (1 MB)");
        System.out.println("  Consumer fetch.max.bytes           : 52428800 bytes (50 MB)");
    }
}
```

```text
Default message size limits:
  Broker  message.max.bytes        : 1048588 bytes (~1 MB)
  Topic   max.message.bytes         : inherits broker default
  Producer max.request.size          : 1048576 bytes (1 MB)
  Consumer max.partition.fetch.bytes : 1048576 bytes (1 MB)
  Consumer fetch.max.bytes           : 52428800 bytes (50 MB)
```

### Why does the default exist?

Kafka is designed for high-throughput streams of many small records. Large messages increase memory pressure, inflate replication traffic, cause latency spikes, and can stall a partition while processed.

### What happens when the limit is exceeded?

```java
package com.bank.error;

public class RecordTooLargeDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Exceeding the limit:");
        System.out.println("  Producer (max.request.size exceeded):");
        System.out.println("    -> RecordTooLargeException at producer");
        System.out.println("    -> Nothing sent to broker");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Broker (message.max.bytes exceeded):");
        System.out.println("    -> Broker rejects produce request");
        System.out.println("    -> Producer sees error");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Consumer (max.partition.fetch.bytes too small):");
        System.out.println("    -> Record stored but consumer CANNOT read it");
        System.out.println("    -> Consumer stalls on that partition");
    }
}
```

```text
Exceeding the limit:
  Producer (max.request.size exceeded):
    -> RecordTooLargeException at producer
    -> Nothing sent to broker

  Broker (message.max.bytes exceeded):
    -> Broker rejects produce request
    -> Producer sees error

  Consumer (max.partition.fetch.bytes too small):
    -> Record stored but consumer CANNOT read it
    -> Consumer stalls on that partition
```

### How do you raise the limits?

All three layers must be raised together.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerConfig;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerConfig;
import java.util.Properties;

public class LargeMessageConfig {
    public static Properties producerConfig() {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ProducerConfig.MAX_REQUEST_SIZE_CONFIG, 5242880);  // 5 MB
        return props;
    }

    public static Properties consumerConfig() {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.MAX_PARTITION_FETCH_BYTES_CONFIG, 5242880);  // 5 MB
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.FETCH_MAX_BYTES_CONFIG, 52428800);  // 50 MB
        return props;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("To support 5 MB messages, raise ALL layers:");
        System.out.println("  1. kafka-configs --alter --topic statements");
        System.out.println("       --add-config max.message.bytes=5242880");
        System.out.println("  2. producer max.request.size = 5242880");
        System.out.println("  3. consumer max.partition.fetch.bytes = 5242880");
        System.out.println("  4. broker replica.fetch.max.bytes = 5242880");
    }
}
```

```text
To support 5 MB messages, raise ALL layers:
  1. kafka-configs --alter --topic statements
       --add-config max.message.bytes=5242880
  2. producer max.request.size = 5242880
  3. consumer max.partition.fetch.bytes = 5242880
  4. broker replica.fetch.max.bytes = 5242880
```

### How does compression help?

```java
package com.bank.config;

public class CompressionForSize {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Compression reduces message size before hitting limits:");
        System.out.println("  Original JSON statement : 3.2 MB");
        System.out.println("  With zstd compression  : 0.6 MB (under 1 MB default!)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  props.put(\"compression.type\", \"zstd\")");
        System.out.println("  Broker stores and ships compressed batch");
        System.out.println("  Consumer decompresses");
    }
}
```

```text
Compression reduces message size before hitting limits:
  Original JSON statement : 3.2 MB
  With zstd compression  : 0.6 MB (under 1 MB default!)

  props.put("compression.type", "zstd")
  Broker stores and ships compressed batch
  Consumer decompresses
```

### What is the claim-check pattern?

For genuinely large payloads like PDFs or images, store the blob in object storage and publish only a small reference on Kafka.

```java
package com.bank.claimcheck;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.KafkaProducer;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerRecord;
import java.util.Properties;

public class ClaimCheckProducer {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Step 1: Upload PDF to S3
        String s3Key = "statements/2024/SAV2001/june.pdf";
        System.out.println("Uploaded PDF to S3: " + s3Key);

        // Step 2: Publish small reference on Kafka
        String reference = "{\"accountId\":\"SAV2001\"," +
            "\"s3Key\":\"" + s3Key + "\"," +
            "\"sizeBytes\":3200000," +
            "\"checksum\":\"abc123\"}";
        System.out.println("Kafka message: " + reference);
        System.out.println("Size: " + reference.getBytes().length + " bytes (tiny!)");
    }
}
```

```text
Uploaded PDF to S3: statements/2024/SAV2001/june.pdf
Kafka message: {"accountId":"SAV2001","s3Key":"statements/2024/SAV2001/june.pdf","sizeBytes":3200000,"checksum":"abc123"}
Size: 117 bytes (tiny!)
```

### What are common mistakes?

```java
package com.bank.mistake;

public class MessageSizeMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG: raise only producer max.request.size");
        System.out.println("  Broker still rejects -> RecordTooLargeException");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("WRONG: raise producer + broker, forget consumer");
        System.out.println("  Record stored but consumer CANNOT read it");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("CORRECT: raise all three layers together");
        System.out.println("  Or better: use claim-check pattern");
    }
}
```

```text
WRONG: raise only producer max.request.size
  Broker still rejects -> RecordTooLargeException

WRONG: raise producer + broker, forget consumer
  Record stored but consumer CANNOT read it

CORRECT: raise all three layers together
  Or better: use claim-check pattern
```

### When should you raise limits vs use claim-check?

| Approach | When to use | Trade-off |
|---|---|---|
| Raise limits | Occasional slightly-over-1MB messages | Changes cluster memory/latency profile |
| Compression | Large but compressible data (JSON) | CPU cost, very effective for text |
| Claim-check | Genuinely large blobs (PDFs, images) | Extra S3 round trip, lifecycle management |

### What should a senior engineer know?

Raising limits is a last resort. The 1 MB default is a deliberate guardrail. Compression is the right first lever for compressible data. The claim-check pattern is the professional solution for large blobs. Any limit change must be load-tested across the cluster.
